# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.2 have nothing from it; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.3 has "resembles_animal" and "actuated_life"; they differ when only "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body" is true, which the first allows
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body" is true and "the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion" is false. Is S1, "Now, if we survey the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, it bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body, and seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion.", true then?
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2 has "resembles_animal ∧ actuated_life → world_animal ∧ deity_soul"; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body", "the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion" and "the world is an animal" are true, which the second allows
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2 takes for granted "if (the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body and the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion), then (the world is an animal and the Deity is the soul of the world, actuating it and actuated by it)"; hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.2 and hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2.3 do not
hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body and the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion), then (the world is an animal and the Deity is the soul of the world, actuating it and actuated by it)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
