# argument written to hume-dialogues-2.logic.r2 by claude-opus-5-5 (a language model, not a rule)
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/invalid: C1 (S1): does not follow: when "a continual circulation of matter in the universe produces no disorder", "a continual waste in every part of the universe is incessantly repaired", "the closest sympathy is perceived throughout the entire system" and "each part or member, in performing its proper offices, operates both to its own preservation and to that of the whole" are true, "the universe, so far as it falls under our knowledge, bears a great resemblance to an animal or organised body" and "the universe seems actuated with a like principle of life and motion" are false, every premise holds and the conclusion fails
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/countermodel: C1 (S1): actuated_life = F, circulation_no_disorder = T, parts_preserve = T, resembles_animal = F, sympathy = T, waste_repaired = T
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/unlinked: P1 (S2): P1 is the only statement about "a continual circulation of matter in the universe produces no disorder", "a continual waste in every part of the universe is incessantly repaired", "the closest sympathy is perceived throughout the entire system" and "each part or member, in performing its proper offices, operates both to its own preservation and to that of the whole": as written it cannot bear on the conclusion; was the same thing named two ways?
hume-dialogues-2.md:1: argue/assumed: C (S3): follows only by A1, which the text never states: state it, or argue for it
